-- title:
--   Point sequence 0→π_*(F⊗ Iₚ)→π_*F→ p^*F→ 0
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a Noetherian commutative ring, and let $c\colon C\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ be proper and smooth of relative dimension $1$, equipped with a section $\varepsilon$, that is, a morphism $\operatorname{Spec}R\to C$ whose composite with $c$ is the identity. Assume chart data of arbitrarily large degree: for every $m_0\in\mathbb N$ there is a `FiniteMapData` for $c$ and $\varepsilon$ with $m\ge m_0$, i.e. two affine opens $U,V$ covering $C$ with $U$ the complement of the image of $\varepsilon$, sections $f\in\Gamma(C,U)$, $g\in\Gamma(C,V)$ with $U\cap V$ equal to both basic opens $C_f$ and $C_g$ and with the restrictions of $f$ and $g$ mutually inverse there, such that $f$ and $g$ make $\Gamma(C,U)$, $\Gamma(C,V)$ finite over $R[x]$, and such that for every local $R$-algebra $S$ and every $s\in S$ the quotient $S\otimes_R\Gamma(C,U)$ by the ideal generated by $1\otimes f-s\otimes 1$ is finite and free of rank $m$ over $S$. Let $t\colon T\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ be locally of finite type, write $\pi=$ `pullback.snd c t` for the base change $C_T\to T$, and let $p\colon T\to C_T$ satisfy $p\circ\pi$-relation $p\ \text{followed by}\ \pi=\mathrm{id}_T$. Let $F$ be a module on $C_T$ which is invertible in the sense that every point has an open neighbourhood $U$ on which the pullback of $F$ along $U\hookrightarrow C_T$ is isomorphic to the unit module, and let $n\in\mathbb N$. Write $I_p=$ `p.ker.module`, the kernel of the canonical map from the unit module to the pushforward of the unit module along the closed subscheme attached to $p$. Assume fibrewise: for every field $k$, every $s\colon\operatorname{Spec}k\to T$ and every cover of the fibre $C_T\times_T\operatorname{Spec}k$ by two affine opens with affine intersection, the two-chart Čech complex of the pullback of $F\otimes I_p$ to that fibre, taken over the structure morphism $\mathrm{fibreAt}$ to $\operatorname{Spec}k$, has subsingleton $H^1$ (the quotient of the sections on the overlap by the image of the Čech differential) and $H^0$ (the kernel of the Čech differential) of $k$-dimension $n$. Then there is a short complex $S$ of modules on $T$ which is short exact and admits isomorphisms $S.X_1\cong\pi_*(F\otimes I_p)$, $S.X_2\cong\pi_*F$ and $S.X_3\cong p^*F$.
--
--   This is the point sequence of Picard-bundle theory: pushing forward $0\to F\otimes I_p\to F\to F|_{p(T)}\to 0$ along $\pi$, where the identification $\pi_*(F|_{p(T)})\cong p^*F$ uses that $p$ is a section, and surjectivity on the right rests on the fibrewise vanishing of $H^1$ for $F\otimes I_p$. It is used in the construction of determinant-of-cohomology comparisons, being cited by [`AlgebraicGeometry.RelPicard.nonempty_det_pushforward_iso_det_pushforward_tensor_idealOfSection_tensor_pullback`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.RelPicard.nonempty_det_pushforward_iso_det_pushforward_tensor_idealOfSection_tensor_pullback).
